course: everyday
lesson: 291
title: Handling anger and everyday fears
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Anger and fear are normal feelings. They are signals, not orders: you can notice them and choose what to do next.
explain: Anger gives early warning signs, like a tight chest or a hot face. The earlier you notice them, the easier it is to pause and calm down, and a calm, clear reason gets listened to far more than a shout or a slammed door.
explain: Fear often shrinks when you talk about it, learn the facts and take small steps. Counting between a lightning flash and the thunder shows a storm moving away; most spiders are harmless; films use special effects.
explain: Example: a boy in Ottawa is scared of his vaccination. He tells the nurse he is nervous, holds his dad's hand, looks away and breathes out slowly. The sting lasts a second, and the vaccine protects him from serious illnesses.
explain: A plan made in advance takes away much of the fear: if your family agrees a meeting place before going into a crowd, you know exactly what to do if you get separated.
fact: When angry, close doors gently; slamming can trap fingers, break things and frighten others.
fact: Use your words once you are calm.
fact: If someone hits you when they are angry, even a brother or sister, tell a parent or another trusted adult; hitting is never OK.
fact: If something is truly unfair, say calmly what is unfair and what you would like to happen instead; people listen to a calm, clear reason more than to a shout.
fact: Early warning signs of anger, like a tight chest or a hot face, are a signal to pause; the earlier you catch anger, the easier it is to calm down.
fact: After a frightening film, talk to a parent and do something calm before bed; films are made up with special effects.
fact: If a needle scares you, tell the nurse, look away, breathe out slowly and hold a hand; the sting lasts a second and the vaccine protects you.
fact: In a thunderstorm, stay indoors away from windows; inside a house you are safe.
fact: Counting the seconds between flash and bang shows whether the storm is moving away: a longer gap means it is going away, a shorter gap means it is coming closer.
fact: Keep your distance from spiders and insects and ask an adult to move them; most are harmless, though in Australia some spiders do need an adult.
fact: Learn deep-water swimming step by step with a teacher, and only swim where an adult is watching.
fact: If the news scares you, ask an adult to explain and limit how much news you watch; the news shows the worst things and often not the helpers.
fact: Before going into a crowd, agree a meeting place; if lost, ask a staff member or a parent with children for help.
exercise: You are furious and want to slam your bedroom door. What should you do? | Close it gently and talk once you are calm | Slam it as hard as you can | Kick it until you feel better | Lock yourself in and refuse to speak to anyone ever again
exercise: Why is slamming a door a bad idea? | It can trap fingers, break things and frighten others | It is only bad because it is noisy for the neighbours | Doors are made to be slammed | It makes the room colder
exercise: Your teacher blames you for something your neighbour did. What is the best response? | Calmly say what is unfair and what you would like to happen | Shout as loudly as you can until the teacher gives up and says sorry | Sulk and refuse to work | Blame someone else instead
exercise: Why does a calm explanation work better than shouting? | People listen to a calm, clear reason much more | Shouting is always ignored by everyone, everywhere, all the time | Calm voices are louder than shouts | Adults cannot hear children who shout
exercise: Which is an early warning sign of anger? | A hot face or a tight chest | Feeling sleepy and yawning | Feeling hungry for lunch | Wanting to laugh at a joke
exercise: Your friend says you get angry too fast. What can help? | Notice your warning signs and pause when you feel them | Hide your anger until you explode | Avoid all your friends | Get angry even faster, so that it is all over a bit sooner
exercise: You watched a scary film and now cannot sleep. What helps? | Talk to a parent and do something calm and cheerful | Watch another scary film | Keep it to yourself and lie awake until it goes away | Hide under the bed all night
exercise: Why is a monster in a film not something to fear in real life? | Films are made up, using special effects | Monsters only ever come out on weekdays, never at weekends | The monster is too far away | Monsters cannot climb stairs
exercise: You are scared of your vaccination needle. What can you do? | Tell the nurse, look away, breathe out slowly and hold a hand | Run out of the room | Refuse all vaccines forever and never go back to the doctor | Watch the needle very closely and hold your breath
exercise: Why is a vaccination worth a small sting? | The sting lasts a second and the vaccine protects you from serious illness | The sting makes you stronger at once, like a superpower in your arm | Vaccines are only for adults | It stops you from ever getting a cold
exercise: A thunderstorm starts while you are at home. Where is safest? | Indoors, away from windows | Outside under a tall, leafy tree | At the open window to watch | On the roof to see it better
exercise: You see lightning and count 10 seconds to the thunder, then later only 4. What does it tell you? | The storm is coming closer | The storm is moving further away | The storm has stopped for good | Nothing at all about the storm
exercise: There is a spider in your bedroom and you are scared. What should you do? | Keep your distance and ask an adult to move it outside | Grab it with your bare hand and throw it out of the window | Scream and jump on the bed | Keep it as a pet in your pocket
exercise: You are scared to swim in the deep end. What is the best way to get brave? | Learn step by step with a teacher, where an adult is watching | Jump in alone when no one is around, to prove you can do it | Swim straight to the deepest part | Never go near a pool again
exercise: News about a big wildfire makes you worried. What helps? | Ask an adult to explain and watch less news | Watch the news all day long so you can keep checking it | Keep your worry secret | Decide the whole world is on fire
exercise: Your family is going to a busy fair. What should you agree before you go in? | A meeting place in case anyone gets lost | Who will get the biggest ice cream at the end of the day | That nobody will hold hands | Which ride to go on last
exercise: You get lost at a theme park. Who should you ask for help? | A staff member or a parent with children | The first grown-up who offers you a lift home | Nobody; just keep walking until you find them | A friendly-looking stranger in the car park
test: A girl in Cork is so angry with her brother that her face is hot and her fists are tight. What should she do first? | Notice the signs and take a pause to calm down | Shout at him straight away | Slam every door in the house until she feels better | Break one of his toys
test: A boy thinks the referee's call was unfair. Which way is most likely to be heard? | Calmly explain why he thinks it is unfair | Throw the ball hard at the referee to show him | Storm off the pitch, shouting at everyone there | Refuse to play again for the rest of the season
test: Which is true about anger? | Catching it early makes it easier to calm down | It is best to let it build up as long as possible | Anger is always wrong to feel | Anger has no warning signs
test: A boy in Florida hears thunder 2 seconds after each flash and it is getting shorter. What does it mean, and what should he do? | The storm is close and coming nearer; stay indoors away from windows | The storm is far away and leaving; go outside and play in the garden | The storm is over; open the windows | Thunder is harmless; stand by the glass door
test: A girl in Perth, Australia, finds a big spider on her shoe. What is wisest? | Keep away from it and ask an adult to deal with it | Pick it up gently to see if it bites people | Squash it with her hand | Put her foot into the shoe anyway
test: A boy saw a frightening scene in a film and keeps thinking about it. Why might talking to his mum help? | Talking about a fear makes it smaller | Talking about it makes the scene come true in real life | His mum will make him watch it all again | Talking only works for adults, not for children
test: A friend is crying before her flu jab. What advice is best? | Tell the nurse, look away and breathe out slowly | Stare at the needle the whole time so it can't surprise her | Hold her breath as long as she can | Run around the waiting room first
test: A boy keeps watching news about war and feels frightened all the time. What would help most? | Watching less news and talking with an adult about it | Watching more news to understand everything that is going on | Keeping the fear a secret | Staying awake to watch the late news
test: A family agrees, "If we get separated, we meet at the big clock." Why does this help? | Everyone knows exactly what to do if it happens | It makes getting lost completely impossible for anyone in the family | Clocks keep children from getting lost | It means children can wander off alone
test: A girl wants to swim in the deep end like her friends but is scared. Which plan is best? | Practise step by step with a teacher while an adult watches | Jump in with her friends when the lifeguard is away | Swim alone at night when nobody can see her practise | Give up swimming for good
test: so mad at my bro i wanna slam the door | Close it gently and talk once you're calm | Slam it, it helps the anger out | Slam it but check his fingers first so nobody's hurt | Kick the door instead of slamming
test: flash then i counted 10, now 15 to the bang. coming or going? | Going away, the gap is getting longer | Coming closer, because the gap is getting longer | Staying still, it's the same storm | You can't tell from counting
test: getting my jab tmrw n im scared | Tell the nurse, look away, breathe out slowly | Run off before they start | Hold your breath and stare right at the needle | Ask to get it next year instead
test: going to the footy final, huge crowd, what if i get lost | Agree a meeting place first, if lost ask staff | Walk around the whole ground till you see your family | Go home on the bus by yourself | Stand in the middle and shout
explain: A boy in Colorado slammed his bedroom door in anger; his little sister's fingers were in the hinge side, and she needed hospital and a splint.
explain: A girl in Australia stood by the window to watch a storm; lightning struck a tree outside and the window shattered, cutting her arm.
fact: Slammed doors crush thousands of children's fingers every year.
fact: Lightning can strike from a storm many kilometres away, so outdoors during thunder is not safe.
test: soooo angry wanna slam my door | Close it gently and calm down first | Slam it, it helps | Kick it really hard instead | Slam it only once
test: thunder but its far away, can we keep swimming | No, get out of the water and go indoors | Yes, till you actually see lightning | Yes, if it's not raining | Yes, water protects you
test: brother hits me when hes mad | Tell a parent; hitting is never OK | Hit him back even harder so he learns | Keep quiet so he doesn't get in trouble | Hide in your room every time he's mad
test: did u hear a kid slammed the door when he was mad and "his little sister's finger came right off". lesson? | Close doors gently even when angry; slams crush fingers | Only slam doors when nobody's near | Slamming helps you calm down faster, so it's worth it | Doors can't really hurt fingers
test: my uncle says lightning "hit a man 20 miles from the storm while the sun was out". what's the bit to trust | Lightning can strike far from the storm, so go indoors when you hear thunder | Lightning only ever strikes in heavy rain, so sunshine means you're safe | You're safe if you can see sun | Lightning never travels far
test: everyone says the boy who cried at his jab is "a wimp lol". pass it on? | No, being scared of needles is normal; don't spread it | Yes, he was being a wimp | Yes, and copy him crying so everyone laughs | Yes, and post it in the chat
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct appropriate during a thunderstorm? | Persons shall remain indoors and away from windows | Persons shall shelter beneath a tall tree to keep dry until it passes | Persons shall remain outdoors until rain begins | Persons shall stand in an open field
exam: Identify the principal benefit of recognising the early signs of anger, such as a tight chest or a hot face. | The earlier anger is noticed, the easier it is to calm down | Such signs indicate that one is ill | Such signs show that the other person is wrong and should apologise | Such signs mean that shouting is justified
exam: It is given that a child perceives a situation as genuinely unfair. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | The child shall state calmly what is unfair and what would help | The child shall shout until heard by every adult in the building | The child shall slam the door and refuse to speak | The child shall retaliate in kind
exam: State the measure appropriate after viewing a frightening film. | Speaking with a parent and doing something calm before bed | Watching a further frightening film | Sleeping with every light switched on all night for a week | Remaining awake until the fear has passed
